<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Sketching A Graph of an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02033</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Sketching A Graph of an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>96c69817-a880-428b-9d82-1659fac64150</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down</title>
      <exercise id="_7a3f6355-50d8-4553-969c-c2510b2fabfc" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex
            of the graph defined by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">3. The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex
            is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex
            of the graph is 3.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a890445c-625a-426a-9e78-080b549637d9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">What is the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex
            of the graph defined by <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">2. In vertex form, the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex
            is the constant. The value of the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate of the vertex
            of the graph is 2.</para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Find the coordinates of two other points on the graph. The points
            should be symmetric across the vertex. Show your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00010"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00012"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>11</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>11</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. When <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is 0, the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> or 11. The other point is
            3 units to the right of the vertex with the same <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-coordinate.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00001">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Draw a graph
            that represents the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
                <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00015">

            <media alt="Graph of a parabola on a coordinate plane. The x-axis has a scale of 1 extending from negative 12 to 12. The vertical-axis extends from negative 24 to 24 with a scale of 2. Three points on the parabola are labeled: (0, 11), (3, 2), and (6, 11).">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_18_09_01.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

    </section>
